<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-family: 'Arial Black', Gadget, sans-serif;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Saving for Future Goals! – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Personal Success Story</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Saving for Future Goals!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reflective essays on personal experiences. Interactive: Exchange essays for peer review on common themes.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can exchange essays with classmates for feedback on personal themes (e.g., "In Amelia's essay, she described overcoming challenges, and I suggested improving the clarity of her conclusion.") Can write a reflective essay on a significant learning experience, relating it to future aspirations (e.g., "Working at the local youth center has taught me leadership skills. If I continue mentoring students, I will build connections that could support my future career in social work.") Can provide constructive peer feedback in written form (e.g., "Your essay is well organized, but I think you could improve the introduction by adding more context.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; font-family: 'Arial Black', Gadget, sans-serif;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students engage with the theme and scenario, activating prior knowledge. Teacher models key language and clarifies meaning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays images of people achieving various goals (e.g., graduating, starting a business, volunteering). Students write down 2-3 words or short phrases that come to mind for each image, focusing on "success," "achievements," or "future aspirations." Then, students briefly share their words with a partner. For example, "This image makes me think of career growth and professional goals."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using target grammar and vocabulary related to personal success stories. For example, "I have volunteered for community service projects, and this experience has broadened my network." (Stress on VOLUNTEERED, COMmunity SERvice, BROAdEned, NETwork). "If I continue to save money, I will be able to invest in my future projects." (Stress on CONtinue, SAVE, inVEST, FUH-ture PROjects). The teacher then provides a simple written example of a reflective sentence on the board.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of experience are we focusing on today – recent, past, or future?" (Expected answer: Both past achievements and future aspirations.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'reflective essay,' what do you think that means? Is it just telling a story, or thinking about its impact?" (Expected answer: Thinking about its impact and personal growth.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students receive new language input connected to the scenario, focusing on model texts for writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher provides students with a short model reflective essay (approx. 150-200 words) titled "My First Steps Towards Financial Freedom," written by a peer (fictional) about their experience saving money and setting career goals. The essay explicitly uses the present perfect, future continuous, and first conditional, along with target vocabulary (e.g., "I have saved some money for my college studies," "In five years, I will be managing my own company," "If I continue my studies, I will find more opportunities," and words like 'finances,' 'investment,' 'career,' 'growth,' 'opportunities').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Narrative excerpt (reflective essay); formal register for an academic context; digital document or handout.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will comprehend the structure and content of a reflective essay on personal experiences and future goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the essay silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (written):</b> "What is the main learning experience described in the essay?" and "What is the author's primary future aspiration?" Students write brief answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases from the essay to their corresponding grammatical function (e.g., "I have managed" – present perfect for achievement; "I will be pursuing" – future continuous for long-term goal; "If I work hard" – first conditional for future success).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students practice new language in controlled and semi-controlled writing activities, focusing on accuracy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Target grammar (Present perfect for achievements, Future continuous for long-term goals, First conditional for future success) and vocabulary (nouns: <i>success, community service, goals, finances, savings, investment, career, projects, growth, initiative, achievements, opportunities, strategies, future</i>; verbs: <i>save, complete, manage, invest, build, broaden, volunteer, pursue, participate, support, work, achieve, aspire, succeed, reflect, continue</i>; adjectives: <i>successful, positive, professional, essential, significant, impactful, financial, careful, long-term, future-oriented, involved, collaborative, personal</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences related to "My Personal Success Story" using the correct form of the verb and a suitable target vocabulary word. Example prompts: "I (achieve) great _____ by (complete) my first ______ project." (Answer: I have achieved great <i>success</i> by <i>completing</i> my first <i>community service</i> project.) "In two years, I (manage) a team, so I (broaden) my skills now." (Answer: In two years, I <i>will be managing</i> a team, so I <i>am broadening</i> my skills now.) "If I (save) enough, I (invest) in my education." (Answer: If I <i>save</i> enough, I <i>will invest</i> in my education.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Paragraph Writing:</b> Students are given three prompts to write three sentences, each using one of the target grammar structures and relevant vocabulary, to form a short paragraph.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: lower-alpha; margin-top: 4px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 1 (Present Perfect): "Describe a personal achievement and its impact." (e.g., "I have initiated a positive project in my neighborhood, which has had a significant impact on local growth.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 2 (Future Continuous): "Talk about a long-term goal you will be working on." (e.g., "Next year, I will be pursuing advanced training to build my professional career.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Prompt 3 (First Conditional): "State a condition for future success related to your goals." (e.g., "If I continue to work hard, I will achieve my financial goals and expand my savings.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Feedback:</b> Students exchange their written paragraphs from activity 2 with a partner. Using a short checklist, they identify if their partner used each grammar structure correctly and incorporated at least three target vocabulary words. They provide written feedback: "You have used the present perfect correctly, and your vocabulary is excellent. I suggest you check the capitalization of 'community'."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students apply language in a comprehensive communicative writing task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario:</b> Students will write a short reflective essay (approx. 150-200 words) titled "My Path to Future Success." They need to describe a significant personal experience or achievement (using present perfect), relate it to their future aspirations (using future continuous), and discuss strategies or conditions for achieving these goals (using first conditional). They must incorporate at least 8-10 target vocabulary words from the list provided (e.g., <i>success, goals, finances, savings, investment, career, growth, opportunities, achieve, aspire, continue, professional, long-term, future-oriented</i>). The essay should respectfully discuss personal achievements and engage with future goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written product (reflective essay).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> Students directly connect their personal experiences to "Saving for Future Goals!" and articulate "My Personal Success Story" through reflection and future planning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> The teacher circulates, offering assistance with vocabulary choice and grammatical structures, prompting students to elaborate on their ideas. For example, "How has volunteering helped you grow?" or "What skills would you like to develop for your future goals?" (Pragmatic function: respectfully discussing achievements and engaging in conversations on goals). The teacher reminds students to consider sentence rhythm and natural flow even in their written expression.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment through peer review and self-reflection on the written product.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their "My Path to Future Success" essays with a different partner. Each student uses a peer review checklist (provided by the teacher) to assess their partner's essay. The checklist includes criteria related to the use of target grammar, vocabulary, clarity of ideas, and overall organization. They provide constructive written feedback directly on the essay or on the checklist. For example, "Your essay is well organized, but I think you could improve the introduction by adding more context." (Learning Outcome: providing constructive peer feedback).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (Are at least 8 target words used appropriately?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> (Correct use of Present Perfect, Future Continuous, First Conditional.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> (Is the essay easy to understand and well-organized?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task completion:</b> (Does the essay reflect on an experience, relate it to aspirations, and discuss strategies?)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Constructive feedback:</b> (Is the peer feedback helpful and respectful?)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following prompts on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One specific aspect of my essay that I am proud of is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One area I want to improve in my future writing is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students and teacher reflect on the lesson's effectiveness and plan for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What challenges did I face when writing my reflective essay about future goals, and how did I try to overcome them?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has this writing lesson helped me better articulate my personal success stories and future aspirations?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What worked:</b> Students actively engaged in peer review and provided thoughtful feedback, demonstrating understanding of grammatical structures and vocabulary. The model essay provided a clear framework.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson:</b> Some students struggled with the coherence of their essays, needing more explicit instruction on paragraph transitions. Consider providing a graphic organizer for essay structure in the future. I need to reinforce the pronunciation focus (stressing content words like 'FINANCES' and 'OPPORTUNITIES') more explicitly in oral instructions or follow-up discussions to help internalize the natural rhythm of these phrases.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will involve students integrating the knowledge and writing skills from this lesson by presenting or summarizing their personal success stories to the class, potentially adapting their essays into a short presentation or creating a visual aid that mediates their written work for a wider audience. This will also involve leading group discussions (Sociolinguistic competence) on saving strategies and professional goals, building on their written reflections.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>